"""))

new_section.append(text_cell("""
**What we noticed.** Performance/body language shows up in 37 of 47 videos (78.7%), compared w/ 11 of 38 photo posts (28.9%). Media/memes go the other way - 10 of 47 videos (21.3%) vs. 27 of 38 photo posts (71.1%). Both gaps are about 49.8 percentage points; they're tied for the biggest difference in the chart.

Food imagery is also more common in photo posts: 16 of 38 (42.1%), compared w/ 5 of 47 videos (10.6%). So the formats do look different in this sample! That doesn't mean changing a post's format would cause its content to change.
"""))

new_section.append(text_cell("""
### 5.4 TikTok: what do the audio values look like?

All 85 posts have the audio measurements below. Tempo is musical speed in beats per minute (BPM). Valence estimates how positive or bright the music sounds; arousal estimates its energy. Both run from 0 to 1. Neither tells us how the creator or viewer feels.

The histograms help us see where values cluster + how spread out they are. Each uses ten equally wide groups of values. The boxplots underneath show the median (middle value) + the middle half of the values. In the table, the 25th + 75th percentiles are the ends of that middle half. The average is the mean; standard deviation describes spread around it. Harmonic ratio describes how much of the audio is tonal rather than noise or percussion.

Valence is the outcome for RQ3, so this helps us understand it before fitting a model. We're looking at audio on its own here, w/o comparing posts w/ vs. w/o restraint labels.
"""))

new_section.append(code_cell("""
# Summarize audio values & give the table readable labels.
audio_columns = [
    'audio_tempo_bpm', 'audio_valence',
    'audio_arousal', 'harmonic_ratio'
]

audio_summary = tiktok[audio_columns].describe().T
audio_summary.index = [
    'Tempo (BPM)',
    'Musical positivity (valence)',
    'Musical energy (arousal)',
    'Tonal share (harmonic ratio)'
]

audio_summary = audio_summary.rename(columns={
    'count': 'Posts counted',
    'mean': 'Average',
    'std': 'Standard deviation',
    'min': 'Lowest',
    '25%': '25th percentile',
    '50%': 'Median',
    '75%': '75th percentile',
    'max': 'Highest'
})

display(audio_summary.round(3))
audio_summary.to_csv(CLEAN / 'audio_summary.csv')

# Histograms show the shape; boxplots show the middle & spread.
plot_columns = [
    'audio_tempo_bpm', 'audio_valence', 'audio_arousal'
]
plot_labels = [
    'Tempo (BPM)',
    'Musical positivity (0–1)',
    'Musical energy (0–1)'
]

figure, charts = plt.subplots(
    2, 3, figsize=(12,6), layout='constrained'
)

for position, column in enumerate(plot_columns):
    values = tiktok[column]
    histogram = charts[0, position]
    boxplot = charts[1, position]

    histogram.hist(values, bins = 10, color=BLUE, edgecolor='white')
    histogram.axvline(values.median(), color=ORANGE, label='Median')
    histogram.set_title(plot_labels[position])
    histogram.set_ylabel('Number of posts')
    histogram.legend()

    boxplot.boxplot(values, orientation='horizontal')

    if column != 'audio_tempo_bpm':
        histogram.set_xlim(0, 1)

    boxplot.set_xlim(histogram.get_xlim())
    boxplot.set_yticks([])
    boxplot.set_xlabel(plot_labels[position])

finish('04_audio_distributions')
"""))

new_section.append(text_cell("""
**What we noticed.** The median tempo is 123.05 BPM. The middle half of posts falls between 112.35 + 143.55 BPM, while the full range is 78.30–172.27 BPM.

For valence, the median is 0.736 but the average is lower at 0.590. The histogram helps explain why - lots of posts have higher scores, but there's also a cluster of lower scores pulling the average down. The middle half spans 0.232–0.774, so one average really doesn't show the whole picture.

Arousal is more tightly grouped, w/ its middle half between 0.303 + 0.385. We shouldn't compare that spread directly w/ tempo's because they use different units. Also, any dots outside the boxplot whiskers are just values flagged by a standard plotting rule; they aren't automatically errors. We kept all the posts!
"""))

new_section.append(text_cell("""
### 5.5 What did we learn + what comes next?

**RQ1 - which concerns + features appear?** Shape concern is the most common main Twitter label (65.0%), followed by eating concern (12.6%) + weight concern (4.4%). Restraint appears in 54.0% of the 326 eating-concern posts - not 54.0% of all related posts. The EDTT plots add a look at its categories, visual features + audio. We keep the datasets separate because the collection methods + labels differ; the percentages can't tell us which platform has "more" of a concern.

**RQ2 - do shape + eating concerns tend to appear together?** We still plan to look at this. Knowing how common each label is doesn't tell us how often they appear together. We haven't made that comparison or run the planned test yet.

**RQ3 - is restraint related to audio valence after accounting for format?** This is still planned too. The visual features differ between formats, + the audio values vary across posts, but that doesn't answer the restraint–valence question. Only 79 posts have the EDE annotations needed for that analysis; the other six stay unknown.

**What this means for the project.** We're keeping our three questions, w/o adding or dropping posts based on these charts. The main things to keep clear are which group each percentage describes, how the six blank EDTT annotations affect the results, + how many posts are behind each format's percentage.

We chose these four figures for specific reasons - label frequencies, the effect of missing annotations, visual features by format, + audio distributions. They help us understand the data before moving on! We haven't run either planned hypothesis analysis, + these files still can't tell us how algorithms or viewing posts affect people's health or attitudes.
"""))

